      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can use the complementary probability approach. This involves calculating the probability that all four dice show the same number and then subtracting this probability from 1.\n\nFirst, let's determine the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four 6-sided dice. Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes is:\n\\[\n6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number. Since each die can show one of 6 faces, there are exactly 6 such outcomes (all dice showing 1, all showing 2, all showing 3, all showing 4, all showing 5, and all showing 6).\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four dice show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{1296} = \\frac{1}{216}\n\\]\n\nThe probability that not all four dice show the same number is the complement of the probability that all four dice show the same number. Thus, we subtract the probability that all four dice show the same number from 1:\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{216}{216} - \\frac{1}{216} = \\frac{215}{216}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{215}{216}}\n\\]",
